=base:main-content.cmark

# Changelog: tramaj-go

Release history of `tramaj-go`, the Go implementation, standard library only. Releases happen when there are
major changes rather than on a schedule; each entry below is one version.
Every implementation has its own page like this one, and each page is
tagged so the site builds one Atom feed per implementation:
#changelog #go #tramaj-go

=base:main-content.tramaj-doc
@version=import("changelog/version", {"package": "tramaj-go"})
.div(class: "changelog",
  $version({
      "version": "0.1.0",
      "date": "unreleased",
      "summary": "Go port: parser, evaluator (concrete and symbolic modes), Node JSON codec, static analyses and a tramaj-go CLI. Standard library only, Go 1.21+.",
      "changes": [
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "Hand-written port checked against \`corpus/cases\` by \`corpus_test.go\`: every case passes in both modes."
          },
          {
              "kind": "changed",
              "text": "Breaking: numbers are two types, an integer and a float (decisions section 18). A literal and a JSON number are typed by their text: \`3\` is an integer, \`3.0\` and \`3e0\` are floats. Integers cover the signed 64-bit range; one outside it is a parse error as a literal and a \`TypeMismatch\` in the context."
          },
          {
              "kind": "changed",
              "text": "Breaking: a number keeps its type when written. \`str(1.0)\` is \`1.0\` where it was \`1\`, and \`CompactJSON\` and \`PrettyJSON\` write a float with a fraction or an exponent."
          },
          {
              "kind": "changed",
              "text": "Breaking: \`eq\` never equates an integer with a float, and \`lt\`/\`lte\`/\`gt\`/\`gte\` take two integers or two floats; a mixed pair is a \`TypeMismatch\`. An array index given to \`has\` or \`lookup\` is an integer."
          },
          {
              "kind": "changed",
              "text": "Breaking: in a \`tramaj.JSON\` value an integer is an \`int64\` and a float a \`float64\`, where every number was a \`float64\`. \`ParseJSON\` types a number by its text and refuses none; \`NormalizeNumbers\` is what refuses an integer beyond 64 bits or a float too large for a double. \`NumberLit\` is now \`IntLit\` and \`FloatLit\`. New: \`FormatInteger\`, \`FormatFloat\`."
          },
          {
              "kind": "changed",
              "text": "Breaking: \`\"$term\"\` is a reserved object key, and \`int\` and \`float\` replace \`number\` as v4 type primitives."
          },
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "The arithmetic profile: \`sum\`, \`product\`, \`negate\`, \`quotient\`, \`inverse\`, \`floor-quotient\`, \`modulo\`, \`floor\` and \`real\`, and the \`NotRepresentable\` error kind. It is off by default: \`Options\`, \`DefaultOptions\`, \`EvalProgramWith\` and \`RunProgramWith\` turn it on, and so does the CLI's \`--arithmetic\`."
          },
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "Terms: over a symbol an arithmetic builtin evaluates to \`{\"$term\": op, \"arguments\": [...]}\`, with its operands as written."
          },
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "\`ArithmeticNames\`, \`ArithmeticOps\` and \`DeepArithmeticOps\`, the arithmetic names a program references; \`tramaj-go analyze arithmetic\` prints them."
          },
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "\`sort-by(list, fn)\` and \`sort-by-descending(list, fn)\` (decisions section 20): a stable sort by a key function, over integer, float or string keys, strings by code point. Both are special forms lowered to one new AST constructor, \`SortBy\`, so neither name can be bound and called or passed by reference."
          },
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "\`format-number(x, decimals, group)\`: a number as positional decimal text, rounding its exact value with ties away from zero, 0 to 20 decimals, never an exponent or a negative zero. An ordinary builtin that needs no option."
          },
          {
              "kind": "added",
              "text": "\`round(x)\`, the tenth name of the arithmetic profile: the nearest integer, ties away from zero. It builds a term over a symbol and is reported by \`ArithmeticOps\`."
          }
      ]
  }).rendered)

